WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.550
The X-Men franchise has weathered plenty of
ups and downs over the years, with the now-iconic

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.610
series playing host to its fair share of garbage,
as well as some of the greatest superhero

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.600
movies ever made.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.620
Here's every one of them, ranked from worst
to best.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.300
In March 2019, Disney finalized its acquisition
of Fox's film assets, and just like that,

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.340
Charles Xavier's School for Gifted Youngsters
was taken over by the House of Mouse.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.529
So when Dark Phoenix hit theaters a few months
later, it was to be the final mainline X-Men

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:31.310
movie of the Fox era, and the last film of
a franchise dating back to the year 2000.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.740
And boy, did it end with a whimper.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.180
Dark Phoenix tells the story of Jean Grey,
a powerful mutant who's possessed by an otherworldly

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.720
and all-consuming entity.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.500
This cosmic force turns Jean into an uncontrollable
force of nature who decides it's time for

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.060
the X-Men to become X-tinct.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.860
And yeah, the movie itself works about as
well as that joke.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:52.000
From its lackluster emotional cues to its
disappointing action scenes, the movie is

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.140
just a total bore.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.600
Sure, you could argue that one other X-Men
movie is technically worse, but at least that

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.820
particular mis-step was good for a laugh.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:00.760
Here, you just end up disappointed.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.040
What could've been a grand space opera filled
with thrilling space action turned out to

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.980
be a dull and disappointing farewell to the
uncanny X-Men.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.780
X-Men Origins: Wolverine was Fox's return
to the X-Men franchise after the original

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.620
trilogy ended in 2006.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.680
But while it's always great watching Hugh
Jackman have fun as Wolverine, even he couldn't

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.020
save this schlockfest from heaps of cheap
CGI, cardboard characters, and a screenplay

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.800
that doesn't even know the difference between
a wolf and a wolverine.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.800
Origins begins in the 19th century and follows
Wolverine through his pre-X-Men days, including

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.210
his relationship with half-brother Sabretooth,
his first love, and even his first dramatic

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.100
yell to the sky.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:37.480
"no."

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:45.500
Unfortunately, the movie is also full of cheesy
dialogue and pointless action scenes, while

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:50.039
the obligatory romance is so rushed that you
feel practically nothing when Logan loses

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.020
the love of his life.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.400
But at least there's still time for this.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.460
"no."

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.820
Adding insult to injury, this is the movie
that stitched up Wade Wilson's mouth, giving

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.320
us a dumb and dull version of Deadpool that
kept Ryan Reynolds out of the X-Men universe

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.100
for seven years.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.200
It's legitimately one of the worst superhero
movies ever made.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:17.020
After two solid entries in the series, the
original X-Men trilogy crashed and burned

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:17.780
with The Last Stand.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.740
Set in the aftermath of Jean Grey's death,
The Last Stand revolves around a young boy

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.960
who develops the ability to "cure" mutants.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.920
Naturally, this doesn't sit well with Magneto,
who raises a ragtag army to kill the kid,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.600
forcing the X-Men to show up and stop him.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.640
The result is a truly messy movie involving
far too many characters.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.560
So many crucial scenes either happen offscreen,
or are glossed over entirely.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.460
Mystique is morphed back into a human and
then basically dropped from the plot.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:47.020
We never see Hank McCoy grapple with his decision
regarding the mutant cure.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:50.540
Cyclops, one of the most important X-Men,
is even murdered off-camera.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.280
But where The Last Stand really falls down
is with the Dark Phoenix herself.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.360
Remove Jean Grey, and you lose pretty much
nothing from the film.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.230
The entire plot should've revolved around
her descent into darkness, but instead she's

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.460
just there to look sexy and make things blow
up.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.390
It just doesn't work, which really makes you
wonder why they decided to revisit this mess

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.320
in Dark Phoenix.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.340
X-Men: Apocalypse actually has a lot going
for it.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.800
The ancient Egyptian intro is pretty exciting,
Magneto's sub-plot could've been its own awesome

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.680
movie, and Quicksilver is, as ever, an absolute
delight.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.900
It's just a shame that the rest of the movie
is such a catastrophe.

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:26.180
The plot involves an ancient deity named,
you guessed it, Apocalypse, who wants to take

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.160
over the world for…reasons.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.220
And while Oscar Isaac occasionally shines
from under those silly-looking prosthetics,

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.400
he's done no favors by a script full of cliched
dialogue and poor motivations.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.150
It doesn't help that there are too many characters
involved in the story too, from Storm and

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.210
Psylocke to Cyclops and Nightcrawler, and
that's not even counting all the old characters

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.060
still around from the previous prequels.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.320
The movie is just too crowded to give every
character their due, and as a result, the

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.920
film falls flat during its big emotional moments.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.650
Thankfully, Fassbender and McAvoy bring their
scenes to life, but the same can't be said

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:00.060
for Jennifer Lawrence, who seems like she
really doesn't want to be there.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.500
The superhero genre was in a weird place in
the year 2000.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.870
A little over a decade before, Tim Burton
had revitalized the genre with Batman, but

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:11.040
after a long line of flops, the genre's popularity
had cooled.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.300
And that's when X-Men came along.

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:16.820
The film was a total hit, taking superhero
movies in a new direction and making things

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:19.009
a little more grounded than they had been
before.

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:22.800
In short, X-Men laid the groundwork for the
modern-day superhero boom, and whether you're

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.719
a Superman fan or an Avengers aficionado,
you probably owe this 2000 flick a debt of

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.320
gratitude.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:29.240
But is it any good?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.840
Well, you've got some dated CGI, a laughable
roar from Sabretooth, and who could forget

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.080
this particular low-point?

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.800
"Do you know what happens to a toad when it's
struck by lightning?"

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.840
Nitpicks aside, however, Bryan Singer's take
on the X-Men is mostly fun, with characters

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:49.289
you actually care about, simple but well-choreographed
action scenes, and the establishment of certain

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.699
figures who quickly became a permanent part
of the Hollywood landscape.

00:04:52.699 --> 00:04:57.039
Ian McKellan is absolutely magnetic as the
metal-bending Magneto, for example, while

00:04:57.039 --> 00:04:59.999
Hugh Jackman is thrillingly feral and savage
as the Wolverine.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:04.629
All in all, X-Men was a fantastic way to start
the franchise…and kick off two whole decades

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:05.840
of superhero films.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.660
Deadpool 2 is kinda like hearing a really
good dirty joke more than once.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.420
The first time, it's shockingly fun.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.240
But the second time, the novelty of that R-rated
edge wears off a little.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.020
Directed by David Leitch, Deadpool 2 finds
the Merc with a Mouth completely lost after

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.520
his girlfriend Vanessa is killed.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.110
But when the indestructible trainee X-Man
comes across a troubled young mutant with

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.920
a fiery temper, Wade Wilson finds new purpose
in his ultra-violent life.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.560
Obviously, there's a lot of meta-humor going
on here.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.200
Pretty much every comic book property in history
is mercilessly roasted, and Wilson takes aim

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.640
at a near-endless number of pop culture targets,
too.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:41.900
The film also introduces some pretty cool
characters, like the ever-lucky Domino and

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.840
arguably the greatest X-Men character of all
time: Peter.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.120
"You're a goddamn superhero, you!

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:47.700
"X-force!"

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:49.440
"X-force."

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:53.900
After the disappointing double whammy of The
Last Stand and X-Men Origins: Wolverine, the

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.920
X-Men franchise was in serious trouble.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.180
Fortunately, director Matthew Vaughn gave
the series a much-needed boost with X-Men:

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.659
First Class, an origin story for the mutant
supergroup.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.880
The crux of the movie revolves around the
contrasting worldviews between Charles Xavier

00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:10.280
and Erik Lehnsherr, who each grew up in radically
different circumstances.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.660
It's idealist versus cynic, and some of the
best scenes of the movie involve Xavier and

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.340
Magneto discussing their opposing perspectives
on humanity.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.500
"We have it in us to be the better men."

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:20.760
"We already are.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.660
We're the next stage of human evolution, you
said it yourself."

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:24.220
"No, no…"

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:29.580
James McAvoy is delightful as Professor X,
and unlike her performance in Apocalypse,

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.120
Jennifer Lawrence is fantastic here, portraying
an earnest Raven Darkholme who struggles with

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:34.800
her own self-esteem.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.400
Speaking of Erik Lehnsherr, Michael Fassbender
as Magneto might be the most inspired bit

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.400
of casting in the franchise since Hugh Jackman's
Wolverine.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:47.020
Fassbender's Magneto is a tragic hero grappling
with the conflict between his desire for revenge

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.200
and his need for a family.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:52.340
His Nazi-hunting scenes are powerful, and
the tense standoff at the South American bar

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:57.009
is almost on par with a certain other classic
scene involving Fassbender, beer, and Nazis.

00:06:57.009 --> 00:07:00.430
Whether he's pulling submarines out of the
ocean or yanking metal fillings from a bad

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:04.059
guy's mouth, Fassbender more than proves his
worth as Ian McKellen's successor.

00:07:05.059 --> 00:07:09.589
Easily the best film in the original trilogy,
X2 begins with one of the most exciting openings

00:07:09.589 --> 00:07:10.800
in superhero history.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.290
And everything is just uphill from there.

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:22.569
The plot follows the X-Men as they confront
one of their nastiest enemies, the mutant-hating

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:24.839
Colonel Stryker, after he kidnaps Xavier.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.740
Desperate, the team is forced to ally with
Magneto and Mystique in an effort to save

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.700
the professor and protect mutantkind.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.120
Every single character gets a moment to shine
here, whether its Pyro facing off with the

00:07:35.129 --> 00:07:39.150
cops, Rogue taking down her fiery friend,
or Storm and Nightcrawler simply discussing

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.499
their views on humanity.

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:44.400
And if you're looking for killer action scenes,
it doesn't stop at the Oval Office beatdown.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.740
The film's best set-pieces include Stryker's
attack on Xavier's mansion, Logan's battle

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.320
with Lady Deathstrike and Magneto's thrilling
escape from his plastic prison.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.800
Action-wise, it doesn't really get much better
than this.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:59.860
Inspired by the 1982 comics series by Chris
Claremont and Frank Miller, The Wolverine

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:03.199
was directed by James Mangold, who later went
on to direct Logan.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.500
And it was the first film to give the mutton-chopped
hero a revisionist spin.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.620
This Logan has lost his cocky swagger.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:13.259
Instead, he's a tired man, living as a hermit
in the mountains, visited nightly by the ghost

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:13.940
of Jean Grey.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.780
All he wants out of life is death.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.439
That changes when he's invited to Japan to
meet a sickly businessman he saved years ago.

00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:24.810
Afraid of dying, the man wants to take Logan's
powers for himself, and in exchange, offers

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:25.969
him an ordinary life.

00:08:25.969 --> 00:08:30.110
But when Logan turns down the deal, he soon
finds himself mysteriously drained of his

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:34.659
regenerative abilities, and is drawn into
a plot involving a corporate heiress, shady

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.300
tycoons, and yakuza thugs.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.880
Drawing inspiration from everything from The
Outlaw Josey Wales to Floating Weeds, James

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:45.380
Mangold crafted a truly unique X-Men movie
and gave a classic comic book hero a whole

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.720
new depth of humanity.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:51.260
Deadpool's first appearance in X-Men Origins:
Wolverine was an absolute disaster.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.420
Thankfully, a few years later Wade Wilson
was back on the big screen.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.140
Only this time, the "Merc with a Mouth" could
drop as many f-bombs as he wanted, there wasn't

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.220
a stitch in sight, and Ryan Reynolds was able
to give the craziest performance of his career.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.930
Directed by Tim Miller, Deadpool tells the
story of special ops soldier-turned-assassin

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.740
Wade Wilson, who is stricken with cancer and
forced to seek radical treatment.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:15.360
Unfortunately, the "cure" leaves him permanently
disfigured, though he does pick up some impressive

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.040
regenerative powers in the process.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:21.060
So after donning a tight red suit, he starts
hunting the evil scientist who destroyed his

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:21.560
life.

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:26.100
Rude, crude, and caked with blood, Deadpool
is a wonderfully self-aware departure from

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.150
your usual superhero flick, which is probably
why it's the highest-grossing R-rated film

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:30.740
of all time.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:36.000
The second installment of the prequel trilogy,
X-Men Days of Future Past is a timey-wimey

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.230
adventure that asks one of sci-fi's most intriguing
questions: Can you change the past?

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:41.780
Or is the future set in stone?

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:46.180
The movie opens in a devastating post-apocalypse,
where mutants are near to being wiped out

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.420
by ever-evolving androids called Sentinels.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.850
These things are impossible to kill, so the
X-Men borrow a page from the SkyNet playbook

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:56.980
and send Wolverine back in time to stop the
Sentinels from ever being created.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:01.190
And if X-Men: First Class was Michael Fassbender's
movie, then Days of Future Past belongs to

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:01.820
James McAvoy.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:06.980
This 1970s Professor X is radically different
from the wise old mutant we know and love.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:11.180
He's angry and depressed, beaten down by life
and tortured by the voices of the dead and

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:11.680
dying.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.240
McAvoy gives Xavier new depths, portraying
him as an unkempt cynic before transforming

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.100
into a kind and compassionate leader.

00:10:18.329 --> 00:10:22.640
Throw in some crazy visuals and a few clever
action scenes, and Days of Future Past becomes

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:25.600
the perfect X-Men film to tie the two main
eras together.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.120
Directed by James Mangold, Logan is a sweaty,
dusty Western, filled with lonely anti-heroes

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:32.320
desperately looking for hope.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:37.579
It's set in the year 2029, when mutants are
supposedly extinct, except for Wolverine,

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:41.660
Professor X, and their albino friend Caliban,
who all live in hiding in rural Mexico.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:43.100
And that's when Laura shows up.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.580
Played to feral perfection by Dafne Keen,
Laura is both a violent animal and a scared

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.800
little girl, constantly hunted down by some
seriously bad dudes.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:55.199
Ever the reluctant warrior, Logan steps back
into his role as hero and, with Xavier in

00:10:55.199 --> 00:10:59.389
the backseat, sets off on a blood-soaked road
trip to get Laura to safety.

00:10:59.389 --> 00:11:03.550
Hugh Jackman's turn as Logan is arguably his
finest performance, not just in the franchise,

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.149
but in his entire career.

00:11:05.149 --> 00:11:09.930
He plays Wolverine as a tired old gunslinger;
bitter, lost, and staring death in the face,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.980
until he's given one last shot at redemption.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.750
Patrick Stewart has never been better than
Professor X in his final days either.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:20.330
And then, of course, there's that final shot,
of a cross tilted into an 'X' atop Logan's

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:24.500
grave, which might just be the most beautiful
and heartbreaking image in X-Men history.

